  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 PM EST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis and Forecast:\n\nUpper level low over the Dakotas will continue to move southeast \ntonight, with an induced sfc low pressure across the Upper Midwest. \nAs the upper level low continues to move east with time on Sunday, \nsfc low will continue eastward and a frontal boundary will work \nthrough northern MI. Right along and ahead of the frontal boundary, a \nband of modest moisture and subsequent precipitation will quickly \nwork from west to east through the state, along with a westerly wind \nshift. Expect light to moderate rain showers with overall rainfall \naccumulations generally ~0.10\" to 0.25\", with precipitation falling \nduring the afternoon and early evening hours for the most part. \nTemperatures prior to the frontal boundary and subsequent wind shift \n(to northwest) during the afternoon hours will be quite mild, with \nupper 50s to low 60s. \n\nThe frontal boundary on Sunday ushers in a much colder airmass to \nbegin the work week. High temperatures on Monday likely do not get \nout of the 30s for many, with lower to mid 40s across Saginaw Bay. \nAdditionally, deep, potent, and positively tilted upper low dives to \nthe south across N MI resulting in lake induced rain/snow showers \nMonday and Monday night. The marginal sfc temps will likely limit \nsnowfall efficiency, especially during the midday and afternoon \nhours on Monday. Be that as it may, the thermodynamic profile is \nquite impressive, with inversion heights up to ~H700 mb. This will \nbe offset in a snow-accumulating sense by the brief window of \nfavorability due to how quickly the upper low ejects off to the east \nalong with quick and erratic low level wind field shifts. Thus, \nthere are competing factors here, but do have an inch or two across \nsome of the northwest snowbelts, at least currently, during the late \nMonday afternoon and evening time frame. Worth keeping an eye on \nthough given the potent upper low passing over the warm lakes. \n\nNext feature(s) to watch for later in the week will be an upper low \ndiving out of the northern Plains and another tracking across the \nsouthern tier of the CONUS. Looks like the general consensus in \nensemble guidance is for a coastal storm to form from the southern \npiece of energy and track into the Mid Atlantic/Northeast, while the \nnorthern upper low impacts N MI with scattered rain and snow \nshowers. If the interaction between these two upper lows is more \ntimely, then more widespread/wetting precipitation will be possible. \nBut for now, scattered rain and snow showers are in the forecast for \nThursday and Friday. Temperatures look to remain a little more \nseasonable, upper 30s to mid 40s for highs through much of this \nupcoming week.",
